{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And it shall be, when YHWH your Elohim brings you, etc.</b> He reminds here that when goodness abounds, one should remember the days of poverty and rebellion, when he was a slave in Egypt, and not forget the Name that brought him out from that servitude to this goodness; rather, he should remember His kindnesses continually and fear Him and serve before Him like a servant before his master. And according to the opinion of our Sages (Ḥullin 17a), he further hints that the good that will be found in the houses that are full is permitted, even if there were things forbidden by the Torah there, such as pots of pork, or vineyards planted as kil'ayim (mixed species) or orlah (uncircumcised fruit), and even in the cisterns it is possible that during their hewing there was a forbidden substance in the plaster within them — or he mentioned the cisterns by way of metaphor to indicate the abundance of goodness, granting them dominion over everything found in the land, whether permitted or forbidden. Now all prohibitions were permitted to them except the prohibition of idolatry, as He will yet explain further (Deuteronomy 7:25): \"You shall not covet the silver and gold on them and take it for yourself, etc.\"; and therefore He mentions here, in the passage that follows (Deuteronomy 7:5): \"Their altars you shall tear down, etc.\" — to destroy idolatry and its appurtenances, while everything else found in the land is permitted. And this permission was in effect until they ate the spoil of their enemies. And some say: during the seven years they conquered, and so it appears in the Gemara in the first chapter of tractate Ḥullin (ibid.). And the Rav wrote in the Laws of Kings and Their Wars (Maimonides, Hilkhot Melakhim 8:1): armed combatants, from the time they enter the territory of the nations and take from among them, are permitted to eat nevelot and trefot and pork and the like, if they are hungry and find nothing to eat except these forbidden foods; likewise they may drink yayin nesekh (wine offered as libation), and from the oral tradition they learned: \"houses full of all good\" — the backs of pigs and the like. And this is not correct, for it was not for the sake of saving a life or hunger alone that it was permitted in time of war, but rather after they conquered the great and good cities and settled in them, He permitted to them the spoil of their enemies; and not for all armed combatants, but only in the land that He swore to our fathers to give us, as is explicitly stated in the matter. Likewise, yayin nesekh that he mentioned is not true, for with all prohibitions of idolatry — the thing itself, its appurtenances, and its offerings — all are forbidden, as it is stated (Deuteronomy 7:26): \"You shall utterly detest it and utterly abominate it, for it is a חֵרֶם (devoted thing).\" And if the Rav intended only to permit their ordinary wine in war — but why would we need this? Torah prohibitions were permitted; would the decrees of their words then be forbidden?"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The phrase וְלֹא מָצָא מַה יֹּאכַל אֶלָּא מַאֲכָלִים אֵלּוּ הָאֲסוּרִים is rendered hyper-literally; the logic (permission only if he finds nothing else) is Ramban's report of Maimonides' position. The final sentence's compressed argument against permitting rabbinic decrees when Torah prohibitions are lifted is translated as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:46:19.835987153Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}